data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"A practical production workflow:\n\n1. **Confirm it’s a leak.** Graph RSS, `heapUsed`, `external`/`arrayBuffers`, and GC behavior over time. A rising RSS alone can be native memory or allocator behavior; a steadily rising post-GC heap is more suggestive of retained JS objects.\n2. **Narrow the scope.** Compare affected instances, traffic, deploys, and routes/jobs. Check whether memory climbs with request volume, a specific workload, or time.\n3. **Capture evidence safely.** On a canary or one drained instance, collect two heap snapshots at different points and compare *retained size* and retaining paths. Snapshots can pause the process and expose secrets or user data—store them securely and avoid broad production capture.\n4. **Use production-friendly diagnostics.** Enable GC/heap telemetry or a profiler on a limited instance; correlate heap growth with request and workload metrics. Avoid forcing GC or attaching a debugger to every instance.\n5. **Fix and verify.** Look for unbounded caches/maps, listeners or timers not removed, queues/buffers accumulating, closures retaining request data, and long-lived references. Re-run the same workload and confirm post-GC heap stabilizes; keep monitoring RSS too.\n\nFor a heap snapshot, Node’s inspector/`v8.writeHeapSnapshot()` can help, but trigger it deliberately on a low-risk instance. If heap stays flat while RSS grows, investigate native addons, `Buffer`/external memory, and allocator behavior."}}

